## Tutorials

With Temporal, you can get today's date and add days in a clear and safe way.

WithJavaScript Date, you only have one type of date object.

Temporal gives youseparate objectsdepending on what you need:

Temporal objects are immutable, which means they cannot be changed after they are created.

Temporal objects return a new valueinstead of modifying the existing one.

DST-safe arithmeticensures time calculations (addition and subtraction) remain accurate across
Daylight Saving Time (DST) transitions, preventing 1-hour errors.

It involves using timezone and calendar-aware objects (ZonedDateTime) that understand
local clock shifts.

RFC 5545(Internet Calendaring and Scheduling Core Object Specification),
is the industry standard for exchanging calendar and scheduling information.

It allows different systems (like Google Calendar, Apple Calendar,
and Microsoft Outlook) to communicate seamlessly.

Use Temporal when you need reliable date and time handling.

### Code Examples

```
// Month 5 is June
let d = new Date(2026, 5, 17);

---

// Create a Date
let d = new Date(2026, 5, 17);
// Add 7 days
d.setDate(d.getDate() + 7);
// Here the original date (d) is lost

---

// Create a Temporal object
const today = Temporal.Now.plainDateISO();
// Add a duration
const nextWeek = today.add({ days: 7 });

---

let d = new Date();
```

## Tutorials

The JavaScriptDate objectis old and has severaldesign problems.

These are the most common mistakes developers make when using Date objects:

In the JavaScript Date object, January is month 0 and December is month 11.

In the JavaScript Temporal object, January is month 1 and December is month 12.

This is much more user-friendly.

JavaScript Date objectscan change.

Date objects aremutable.

JavaScript Temporal objectscannot change.

Temporal objects areimmutable.

JavaScript has beena minefield of different formats, browser quirks and locale surprises.

Date strings can be parsed differently across browsers and environments,
and different formats may be interpreted as local time or UTC.

A date string like2026-05-07is interpreted asUTC timein JavaScript.

A date-time string like2026-05-07T14:40:00is interpreted aslocal time.

Temporal avoids the problems above by defining strict parsing rules for
ISO strings.

Temporal.PlainDateis not a timestamp.

It is just2026-05-17with no time and no time zone.

new Date(2026, 4, 1)creates a timestamp of
your local time at midnight.

This means a day can "shift" when you format it to UTC.

A Date object always stores a timestamp, but it can display values as local time or UTC.

This mix can easily cause confusion when converting and comparing dates.

The same moment can look different depending on how it is displayed.

Temporal uses clear types for time zones.

Temporal.ZonedDateTimealways includes a time zone.

Daylight saving time (DST) can cause a day to be 23 or 25 hours long.

This can break calculations that assume every day is exactly 24 hours.

The result can be off by one hour in time zones with DST changes.

Date math often requires manual calculations.

Temporal has built-in date arithmetic.

Date mixes everything in one object.

Temporal objectsseparate different use cases into different objects.

This makes code easier to read and less error-prone.

